response:
 Since they each eat some of the remaining cookie, the fraction remaining must be less than $\frac{2}{7}$, and less than $\frac{3}{5}$. If we simplify $\frac{2}{7}$ and $\frac{3}{5}$, the common denominator is 35. The common denominator is well-suited since the denominators of 7 and 5 are both factors of 35, and 35 is the smaller common denominator. We get \[\frac{2}{7}\cdot\frac{5}{5}=\frac{10}{35}\] \[\frac{3}{5}\cdot\frac{70}{70}=\frac{21}{35}\] Since the fractions have common denominators, we can easily subtract them from each other. \begin{align*}
\frac{3}{5}-\frac{2}{7} &= \frac{21}{35} - \frac{10}{35} \\
&= \frac{3\cdot7-2\cdot5}{35} \\
&= \frac{7}{35} \\
&= \boxed{\frac{1}{5}}
\end{align*} </think> <answer> \frac{1}{5} </answer>

