 First, we draw the altitude of the triangle to create two 30-60-90 right triangles.  [asy]
unitsize(6mm);
defaultpen(linewidth(.7pt)+fontsize(8pt));

real r=2*sqrt(3);

pair A=r*dir(0), B=r*dir(60), C=r*dir(180), F=foot(B,A,C);
draw(A--B--C--cycle);
draw(rightanglemark(A,B,C,8));
draw(B--F,linetype("4 2"));
label("3 units",waypoint(B--F,0.6),W);
[/asy]Let H be the foot of the altitude.  The altitude AH divides triangle ABC into right triangle BHC and right triangle CHA.  Since <math> \angle B = 60^\circ </math> and triangle BHC is an angle-angle triangle similar to an equilateral triangle, HC must be half the length of BC, which means CH = 3.  Also, since Triangle CHA is an 30-60-90 triangle, CH cannot be longer than AC, so AC must be 3. Finally, H is the midpoint of the hypotenuse, so HA must be half the length of the hypotenuse, which means $H$ is 1.5 units below B: $$AH = 3/2 =1.5$$  Using right triangle BHC, we find BC: $$ 1.5/BC = \cos 60^\circ = \frac{1}{2}$$ $$BC = 1.5/0.5=3$$  Triangle ABC is thus a 30-60-90 right triangle with hypotenuse $\sqrt{3}$ times the shorter leg.  Hence: $$AB=BC\sqrt{3}=3\sqrt{3}$$  The area of triangle ABC is: $$\frac{3\times3\sqrt{3}}{2}=\boxed{\frac{9}{2}\sqrt{3}}$$ </think> <answer> \frac{9}{2}\sqrt{3} </answer>

 The $\textbf{Arithmetic Mean(AM)}$, of the given arithmetic sequence is: \begin{align}
\text{AM} &= \frac{1 + 10 + 19}{3} = \frac{30}{3} \\
